B. Why hard cutover (Decision #4) over a feature-flagged switchover
-------------------------------------------------------------------

  Decision #4 of phase-42a's locked decisions: hard cutover, no flag.
  Rationale:

  - A flag leaves dead code at the legacy entry point indefinitely.
    Every future contributor has to ask "which path is live?" and the
    answer depends on env at runtime, not on the source.
  - Observability splits across two pipelines. Two sets of metrics,
    two sets of dashboards, two sets of alerts. Operationally heavier
    than the cutover risk it claims to mitigate.
  - The only test that genuinely matters (production traffic on the
    new pipeline) is deferred indefinitely under a flag. Without
    production traffic, the new pipeline accumulates rot.
  - Phase-42's reflective TestSinglePipelineInvariant ALREADY asserts
    there is no second pipeline. A flag would either subvert that test
    (by gating which pipeline the test sees) or be incompatible with
    it (the test would fail under either flag value while both paths
    coexist). Both outcomes are worse than a hard cutover.
  - Rollback is a one-line revert of the cutover commit. The risk of
    "we cannot roll back" is mitigated by having ONE commit that did
    the cutover, not by having a flag that requires careful unflipping.

C. Why ADR-004 #4 is being reversed (Decision #7)
-------------------------------------------------

  User-stated invariant: no UI feature may be deleted as collateral
  damage of a backend refactor. Phase-43 audit (prompt 0080) found 9
  hooks with broken URLs, 6 of which call endpoints that read from
  tables phase-42 dropped without replacement. Under the original
  ADR-004 #7 ("no backfill — start fresh on deploy"), those frontend
  features would either silently 404 or be removed. The user's
  invariant rules out both outcomes.

  The amendment narrows the no-backfill rule: data is still not
  backfilled (per the original cost/correctness argument), but every
  dropped feature must have a replacement endpoint sourced from the
  new SI schema (signal_log, fsm_live, drives_si, trips, etc.). The
  replacement endpoints are scoped to phase-43a (a separate slate),
  not phase-42a, because they cannot be authored until the new SI
  tables are actually receiving writes — which is phase-42a's job.

D. Why phase-43a is sequenced AFTER phase-42a (data flow dependency)
--------------------------------------------------------------------

  Phase-43a's replacement handlers query the new SI tables. Those
  tables have schema (migrations 000181-000188 from phase-42) but no
  data flowing in until phase-42a wires up writers + cutover. Authoring
  phase-43a handlers before phase-42a finishes would mean writing
  handlers against empty tables, which:

  - Cannot be tested end-to-end (handler returns 0 rows; cannot
    distinguish broken handler from missing data).
  - Cannot be safely shipped (operator would deploy and see all
    replacement endpoints return empty, indistinguishable from "the
    feature is broken").
  - Forces phase-43a to maintain test fixtures that double-shadow
    what phase-42a's writers will produce, creating drift risk.

  Sequencing phase-43a AFTER phase-42a means phase-43a handlers can
  be tested against a production-shaped data stream and shipped with
  confidence.

